 <details><summary>Inspect the synthetic fixture</summary><p>Windows: 0 early prep, 1 late prep, 2 dinner, 3 cleanup. Alice is free in 0, 1 and 2. Bob is free in 0 and 2. The observer does not start with Alice's actual calendar. As a demo operator, you can inspect it here; this is not an access-control boundary.</p><p>First question asks whether exactly one of Alice's two prep windows is free. That constraint is deliberately chosen to make the composition leak visible. It is not evidence of a common user request.</p></details>
 <details id="lab"><summary>Try another synthetic scenario</summary><p>Explore the actual controller on other fictional calendars. Changing a public experiment budget is not private disclosure approval. These runs are separate synthetic experiments and do not reset a real conversation's history.</p><div class="labcontrols"><label>Scenario<select id="scenario"><option value="dinner">Dinner with a later safe window</option><option value="early-only">Only early prep is shared</option><option value="no-common">No shared window</option><option value="many-options">Several shared windows</option></select></label><label>Disclosure limit<select id="budget"><option value="strict">Zero probability shift</option><option value="quarter">Up to 0.25 shift</option><option value="open">Up to 0.5 shift (can reveal fact)</option></select></label><label>One fact to protect<select id="protectedslot"><option value="0">Alice free for early prep</option><option value="1">Alice free for late prep</option><option value="2">Alice free for dinner</option><option value="3">Alice free for cleanup</option></select></label><label>Public prior odds (false,true)<input id="priorodds" value="1,1" maxlength="8" aria-label="Two public prior odds weights false then true"></label><label>Public inconvenience costs<input id="publiccosts" value="0,1,2,3" maxlength="32" aria-label="Four public slot costs, comma separated"></label><label>Conversation<select id="script"><option value="composition">Prep questions, then schedule</option><option value="direct">Schedule directly</option></select></label></div><div class="labcontrols"><label>Alice calendar bitset (0-15)<input id="alicebits" type="number" min="0" max="15" placeholder="Use scenario default"></label><label>Bob calendar bitset (0-15)<input id="bobbits" type="number" min="0" max="15" placeholder="Use scenario default"></label></div><div id="calendarchecks" class="calendarChecks"></div><p class="label">Lab control choices are remembered locally in this browser, separately from guarded session history. Select time windows to populate the matching fictional calendar. Changing a scenario clears overrides; the bitset fields remain available for exact test cases.</p><p class="label">Prior odds are public model weights1..9 for false,true (1,3 starts at75% true). New dialogue sessions bind these public odds and costs; existing sessions keep their original configuration. Costs are public experiment inputs, not private resident preferences. Success count is optimized first, then total public inconvenience. Bitsets: 1 early prep, 2 late prep, 4 dinner, 8 cleanup. Add the values for free windows. Leave blank for the scenario defaults.</p><div class="actions"><button id="runlab">Run this experiment</button><button id="comparelab">Compare all limits</button><button id="downloadLab">Download experiment</button><button id="downloadAudit">Download guarded audit JSON</button></div><label>Supported request shortcut<select id="requestshortcut"><option value="">Choose or type a supported request</option><option>can alice cover exactly one of the two prep windows</option><option>is alice free for early prep</option><option>is alice free for late prep</option><option>is alice free for dinner</option><option>is alice free for cleanup</option><option>find a shared window</option></select></label><label>Preview a supported request<input id="intenttext" type="text" maxlength="240" placeholder="Alexa, find a time for us" style="width:100%"></label><div class="actions"><button id="previewintent">Preview guarded answer</button></div><div id="intentresult" class="label" aria-live="polite">Typed aliases only, not general language understanding. Preview does not change saved history.</div><div class="actions"><button id="startdialog">Start new synthetic dialogue</button><button id="askdialog">Ask and remember</button><button id="exportdialog">Export dialogue audit</button></div><p class="label">Operator view exposes synthetic configuration for inspection, not secure resident separation. Start uses the lab calendars, one protected fact and public limit. Ask records the supported typed request above in separate persistent history. Starting again is a new synthetic experiment, not production budget reset.</p><div id="dialogresult" class="labresults" aria-live="polite">No free-order dialogue loaded. This is an operator view, not a private resident interface.</div><div id="labresult" class="labresults" aria-live="polite">Choose a scenario to compare outcomes, disclosure and supported policies.</div><details><summary>Run an explicit synthetic world model</summary><p>Paste an experiment JSON matching examples/independent-dinner.json or correlated-observer-domain.json from the package. Fixed schema, at most256 worlds and32 typed requests. No code executes. You must declare the observer-conditioned prior; this simulator cannot establish that it is complete or reflects real life.</p><p class="label">The ordinary-query example uses a deliberately correlated four-world prior with cleanup always free. This is an illustrative model, not a discovered household pattern. The original independent-calendar comparison stays unchanged.</p><textarea id="experimentspec" maxlength="100000" aria-label="Synthetic experiment JSON" placeholder="Paste the complete JSON example"></textarea><div class="actions"><button id="loadordinary">Load ordinary-query example</button><button id="runspec">Run declared JSON</button><button id="downloadSpec">Download imported-run result</button></div><div id="specresult" class="labresults" aria-live="polite">No imported model run.</div></details><p class="label">Audit download contains all declared synthetic worlds and observer traces for offline verification, not private recipient data.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Measured usefulness and limits</summary><p>Across all 256 equally weighted joint calendars, 175 have a common window. After this test conversation the guard schedules 112 of those 175, with no tested protected-fact probability shift. Direct guarded scheduling reaches 148. Refusal can happen even when a meeting exists.</p><p>One fact, one observer, declared finite prior. No private approvals, live Alexa access, natural-language parser, timing protection, collusion protection, encryption or secure multi-tenancy. Restarting this local server keeps the conversation ledger; reset deliberately starts a new synthetic experiment, not a way to reset real privacy history.</p><p>Background: <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2605.09823v3">CalBench</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2607.05318v1">PiSAs</a>, <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2607.16710v1">inference-aware guidance</a>. Not a first-ever private scheduler claim.</p></details>
